  publisher = "Elsevier BV",
  volume    =  27,
  number    =  9,
  pages     = "1925--1935",
  abstract  = "BACKGROUND: Optimal treatment of anal squamous cell carcinoma
               (ASCC) is definitive chemoradiation. Patients with persistent or
               recurrent disease require abdominoperineal resection (APR).
               Current models for predicting need for APR and overall survival
               are limited by low accuracy or small datasets. This study sought
               to use machine learning (ML) to develop more accurate models for
               locoregional failure and overall survival for ASCC. METHODS: This
               study used the National Cancer Database from 2004-2018, divided
               into training, validation, and test sets. We included patients
               with stage I-III ASCC who underwent chemoradiation. Our primary
               outcomes were need for APR and 3-year overall survival. Random
               forest (RF), gradient boosting (XGB), and neural network (NN)
               ML-based models were developed and compared with logistic
               regression (LR). Accuracy was assessed using area under the
               receiver operating characteristic curve (AUROC). RESULTS: APR was
