               we show that UTMC (f = 1 MHz; PNP = 250 kPa; pulse length = 10
               μs; pulse interval = 10 ms; treatment duration = 10 s) rapidly
               activates a mechanotransduction pathway initiated by
               piezo1-mediated Ca2+ influx, subsequently activating endothelial
               nitric oxide synthase (eNOS), leading to increased nitric oxide
               (NO) production and widespread protein S-nitrosylation. The
               S-nitrosylation includes reversible modification of VE-cadherin,
               a core component of endothelial adherens junctions that link
               adjacent endothelial cells. Together, these changes cause
               inter-endothelial gaps and a brief increase in paracellular
               permeability without permanently compromising endothelial
               integrity. Importantly, junctional structure and gene expression
               changes return to baseline within 24 h, as shown by bulk RNA
               sequencing, indicating barrier recovery. Overall, our results
               describe a piezo1-Ca2+-eNOS-NO signaling axis involved in
